WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.517
I go to a lot of live shows,

00:00:02.557 --> 00:00:05.527
so I built Onda: a diary for those nights.

00:00:05.567 --> 00:00:07.050
I designed it, built it,

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:08.689
and run it in production.

00:00:08.729 --> 00:00:10.365
Here is the whole system,

00:00:10.405 --> 00:00:12.224
one tap at a time.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:16.459
Friday. Maya opens Onda to find a show.

00:00:16.499 --> 00:00:18.135
Where do the events come from?

00:00:18.175 --> 00:00:20.497
From a website I do not control.

00:00:20.537 --> 00:00:23.011
So Onda trusts nothing it sends.

00:00:23.051 --> 00:00:24.688
Each night it fetches the listings

00:00:24.728 --> 00:00:26.888
inside hard limits.

00:00:26.928 --> 00:00:28.792
It stores every raw response

00:00:28.832 --> 00:00:30.773
before any code reads it.

00:00:30.813 --> 00:00:33.364
Then each listing gets its own transaction.

00:00:33.404 --> 00:00:35.345
A valid one becomes an event.

00:00:35.385 --> 00:00:38.164
A broken one leaves only a rejection record.

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:40.652
The website's identifiers stop at a map,

00:00:40.692 --> 00:00:42.164
so I can replay any night

00:00:42.204 --> 00:00:44.747
and nothing is created twice.

00:00:44.947 --> 00:00:46.761
Maya taps an event.

00:00:46.801 --> 00:00:49.250
Her request never waits for that website.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.307
It reads my own database.

00:00:51.347 --> 00:00:53.516
One server, three containers.

00:00:53.556 --> 00:00:56.594
Only the proxy faces the internet.

00:00:56.794 --> 00:00:58.659
Saturday, she goes.

00:00:58.699 --> 00:01:00.640
Sunday, she rates the night:

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.316
four and a half stars.

00:01:02.356 --> 00:01:05.280
A diary entry has three states.

00:01:05.618 --> 00:01:07.788
Every write passes four guards.

00:01:07.828 --> 00:01:09.388
The last one is the schema,

00:01:09.428 --> 00:01:11.521
so no code path can skip it.

00:01:11.561 --> 00:01:13.197
Two taps at the same instant

00:01:13.237 --> 00:01:15.361
still make one row.

00:01:15.561 --> 00:01:17.959
Now her friend Leo opens Home.

00:01:17.999 --> 00:01:20.499
There is no feed table to keep in sync.

00:01:20.539 --> 00:01:22.073
Home is one query.

00:01:22.113 --> 00:01:24.511
Five branches, each filtered for privacy,

00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:25.959
then one sort.

00:01:25.999 --> 00:01:28.321
His cursor is the key of the last row,

00:01:28.361 --> 00:01:30.759
so new activity never shifts his page.

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:32.283
At 100,000 rows,

00:01:32.323 --> 00:01:35.361
a page takes 88 milliseconds.

00:01:35.837 --> 00:01:37.295
I know where this stops scaling,

00:01:37.335 --> 00:01:40.018
and the docs say what I would try next.

00:01:41.218 --> 00:01:43.083
Maya plans her next show.

00:01:43.123 --> 00:01:45.216
Then the website drops that listing.

00:01:45.256 --> 00:01:46.435
Is the show cancelled,

00:01:46.475 --> 00:01:48.437
or did the fetch fail?

00:01:48.477 --> 00:01:49.554
A miss only counts

00:01:49.594 --> 00:01:51.535
when the whole snapshot arrived.

00:01:51.575 --> 00:01:53.364
Three misses hide the event.

00:01:53.404 --> 00:01:55.192
One sighting brings it back.

00:01:55.232 --> 00:01:56.259
Nothing is deleted,

00:01:56.299 --> 00:01:59.109
and a past night never changes.

00:01:59.309 --> 00:02:00.945
And if I ship a bad change,

00:02:00.985 --> 00:02:02.469
or the server dies?

00:02:02.509 --> 00:02:05.356
Every change passes five checks.

00:02:05.594 --> 00:02:07.535
Only the tested commit deploys,

00:02:07.575 --> 00:02:08.500
after a backup,

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:10.126
with a health check behind it.

00:02:10.166 --> 00:02:10.786
Every day,

00:02:10.826 --> 00:02:13.097
an encrypted backup leaves the server.

00:02:13.137 --> 00:02:15.230
Every month, a job restores one,

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.897
because an untested backup is only a hope.

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:19.726
It is still one server.

00:02:19.766 --> 00:02:22.164
A bad day can cost a day of data.

00:02:22.204 --> 00:02:24.424
At this size, that is the right price,

00:02:24.464 --> 00:02:27.223
and the docs say when it stops being right.

00:02:28.223 --> 00:02:30.240
Trust nothing from outside.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.221
Keep every write atomic.

00:02:32.261 --> 00:02:34.278
Derive instead of copying.

00:02:34.318 --> 00:02:35.954
Prove what you run.

00:02:35.994 --> 00:02:38.545
Sixteen decisions, each with its cost,

00:02:38.585 --> 00:02:40.145
are in the repository.

00:02:40.185 --> 00:02:42.004
Thank you for your time.

